<mxfile host="app.diagrams.net">
  <diagram name="Builder Pattern" id="builder">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1434" dy="844" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1600" pageHeight="1600">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" />
        <mxCell id="1" parent="0" />
        
        <!-- Title -->
        <mxCell id="title" value="BUILDER DESIGN PATTERN - Step-by-Step Object Construction" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=20;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="20" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- PROBLEM -->
        <mxCell id="problem_title" value="THE PROBLEM: Objects with Many Optional Parameters" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="70" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="problem" value="SCENARIO: Student class with many fields&#xa;&#xa;class Student {&#xa;  int rollNumber;      // MANDATORY&#xa;  int age;             // Optional&#xa;  String name;         // Optional&#xa;  String fatherName;   // Optional&#xa;  String motherName;   // Optional&#xa;  String subjects;     // Optional&#xa;  // ... 50+ more optional fields in real projects!&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;PROBLEM 1: HUGE CONSTRUCTOR PARAMETER LIST&#xa;  Student(int roll, int age, String name, String father, String mother, String subjects, ...)&#xa;  ✗ Unreadable, error-prone&#xa;  ✗ Hard to remember parameter order&#xa;  ✗ What if only rollNumber + name needed?&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;PROBLEM 2: MULTIPLE CONSTRUCTORS (Constructor Overload Hell)&#xa;  Student(int roll)&#xa;  Student(int roll, int age)&#xa;  Student(int roll, int age, String name)&#xa;  Student(int roll, String fatherName, String motherName)  // ✗ COMPILE ERROR!&#xa;  Student(int roll, String name)  // Same signature as above! ✗&#xa;  &#xa;  ✗ Too many constructors (combinatorial explosion)&#xa;  ✗ Can't create: Student(rollNumber, motherName) if Student(rollNumber, studentName) exists&#xa;     (both have same signature: int, String)&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;PROBLEM 3: IMMUTABILITY + VALIDATION&#xa;  Setters break immutability&#xa;  Constructor with 50 params → unreadable&#xa;  How to validate before construction?&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;SOLUTION: BUILDER PATTERN!&#xa;  ✓ Step-by-step object creation&#xa;  ✓ Readable: student.setRollNumber(1).setAge(20).setName('John').build()&#xa;  ✓ Only set what you need (optional params)&#xa;  ✓ Immutable final object after .build()&#xa;  ✓ Validation before construction" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#f8cecc;strokeColor=#b85450;fontSize=10;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="110" width="1520" height="560" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- SOLUTION -->
        <mxCell id="solution_title" value="THE SOLUTION: Builder Pattern Step-by-Step" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="700" width="740" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="solution" value="BUILDER PATTERN LAYERS:&#xa;&#xa;1. PRODUCT (Student) - Final object we want&#xa;2. BUILDER (Interface/Abstract) - Defines steps&#xa;3. CONCRETE BUILDER (EngineerStudentBuilder, MBAStudentBuilder)&#xa;4. DIRECTOR - Orchestrates building process (optional)&#xa;5. CLIENT - Uses Director/Builder to get Product&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;STEP-BY-STEP CREATION:&#xa;&#xa;  builder.setRollNumber(1)    → Returns Builder (mediator form)&#xa;         .setAge(20)          → Returns Builder (mediator form)&#xa;         .setName('John')     → Returns Builder (mediator form)&#xa;         .setSubject('DSA')   → Returns Builder (mediator form)&#xa;         .build();            → Returns STUDENT (final object)&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;MEDIATOR FORM:&#xa;  After each step (setXXX), object is in Builder form&#xa;  NOT final Student yet!&#xa;  Only .build() returns final Student&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;REAL EXAMPLE: StringBuilder!&#xa;  StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();&#xa;  sb.append('Hello')   // Returns StringBuilder (mediator)&#xa;    .append(' ')       // Returns StringBuilder (mediator)&#xa;    .append('World')   // Returns StringBuilder (mediator)&#xa;    .toString();       // Returns String (final object)&#xa;  &#xa;  Same pattern! Each append() returns StringBuilder.&#xa;  Only toString() gives final String.&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;HOUSE BUILDING ANALOGY:&#xa;  addWall()    → House in construction (not complete)&#xa;  addRoof()    → House in construction (not complete)&#xa;  addDoor()    → House in construction (not complete)&#xa;  addWindow()  → House in construction (not complete)&#xa;  build()      → Complete House! (final object)&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;BENEFITS:&#xa;  ✓ Readable fluent API (method chaining)&#xa;  ✓ No constructor parameter explosion&#xa;  ✓ Handles optional parameters elegantly&#xa;  ✓ Immutable final object&#xa;  ✓ Can have multiple builders (Engineer, MBA)&#xa;  ✓ Director can enforce sequence&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;DISADVANTAGE:&#xa;  ✗ Code duplication (Student + StudentBuilder have same fields)&#xa;  ✗ More lines of code (trade-off for readability)" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=10;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="740" width="740" height="740" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- CODE EXAMPLE -->
        <mxCell id="code_title" value="CODE IMPLEMENTATION" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="820" y="700" width="740" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="code" value="// 1. PRODUCT (Final object)&#xa;class Student {&#xa;  // All fields (mandatory + optional)&#xa;  private int rollNumber;  // Mandatory&#xa;  private int age;&#xa;  private String name;&#xa;  private String fatherName;&#xa;  private String motherName;&#xa;  private String subjects;&#xa;  &#xa;  // Constructor takes BUILDER (not individual params!)&#xa;  public Student(StudentBuilder builder) {&#xa;    this.rollNumber = builder.rollNumber;&#xa;    this.age = builder.age;&#xa;    this.name = builder.name;&#xa;    this.fatherName = builder.fatherName;&#xa;    this.motherName = builder.motherName;&#xa;    this.subjects = builder.subjects;&#xa;  }&#xa;  // Getters only (immutable)&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;// 2. BUILDER INTERFACE (defines steps)&#xa;interface StudentBuilder {&#xa;  StudentBuilder setRollNumber(int rollNumber);&#xa;  StudentBuilder setAge(int age);&#xa;  StudentBuilder setName(String name);&#xa;  StudentBuilder setFatherName(String fatherName);&#xa;  StudentBuilder setMotherName(String motherName);&#xa;  StudentBuilder setSubjects(String subjects);&#xa;  Student build();  // Returns final Student&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;// 3. CONCRETE BUILDER (Engineer Student)&#xa;class EngineerStudentBuilder implements StudentBuilder {&#xa;  // Same fields as Student (CODE DUPLICATION!)&#xa;  private int rollNumber;&#xa;  private int age;&#xa;  private String name;&#xa;  private String fatherName;&#xa;  private String motherName;&#xa;  private String subjects;&#xa;  &#xa;  public StudentBuilder setRollNumber(int rollNumber) {&#xa;    this.rollNumber = rollNumber;&#xa;    return this;  // Returns Builder (for chaining)&#xa;  }&#xa;  &#xa;  public StudentBuilder setAge(int age) {&#xa;    this.age = age;&#xa;    return this;&#xa;  }&#xa;  &#xa;  public StudentBuilder setName(String name) {&#xa;    this.name = name;&#xa;    return this;&#xa;  }&#xa;  &#xa;  public StudentBuilder setSubjects(String subjects) {&#xa;    // Engineer-specific subjects&#xa;    this.subjects = 'DSA, OS, Computer Architecture';&#xa;    return this;&#xa;  }&#xa;  &#xa;  public Student build() {&#xa;    // Create final Student object&#xa;    return new Student(this);&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;// 3. CONCRETE BUILDER (MBA Student)&#xa;class MBAStudentBuilder implements StudentBuilder {&#xa;  // Same fields...&#xa;  &#xa;  public StudentBuilder setSubjects(String subjects) {&#xa;    // MBA-specific subjects&#xa;    this.subjects = 'Economics, Business, Operations';&#xa;    return this;&#xa;  }&#xa;  &#xa;  public Student build() {&#xa;    return new Student(this);&#xa;  }&#xa;  // Other methods similar...&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;// 4. DIRECTOR (orchestrates building - OPTIONAL)&#xa;class Director {&#xa;  private StudentBuilder builder;&#xa;  &#xa;  public Director(StudentBuilder builder) {&#xa;    this.builder = builder;&#xa;  }&#xa;  &#xa;  // Director defines the SEQUENCE&#xa;  public Student createStudent() {&#xa;    return builder&#xa;        .setRollNumber(1)      // Step 1&#xa;        .setAge(20)            // Step 2&#xa;        .setName('John')       // Step 3&#xa;        .setSubjects('...')    // Step 4&#xa;        .build();              // Build!&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;// 5. CLIENT (uses Director or Builder directly)&#xa;class Client {&#xa;  public static void main(String[] args) {&#xa;    // Option 1: Use Director&#xa;    Director director = new Director(new EngineerStudentBuilder());&#xa;    Student engineer = director.createStudent();&#xa;    &#xa;    // Option 2: Use Builder directly&#xa;    Student student = new EngineerStudentBuilder()&#xa;        .setRollNumber(1)&#xa;        .setAge(20)&#xa;        .setName('Alice')&#xa;        .build();&#xa;  }&#xa;}&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;KEY POINTS:&#xa;  ✓ Each setXXX() returns 'this' (Builder instance)&#xa;  ✓ Enables method chaining (fluent API)&#xa;  ✓ build() returns final Student object&#xa;  ✓ Student constructor takes Builder (not 50 params!)&#xa;  ✓ Student is immutable (no setters)" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#dae8fc;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;fontSize=9;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="820" y="740" width="740" height="1280" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- BUILDER VS DECORATOR -->
        <mxCell id="vs_title" value="BUILDER vs DECORATOR (Pizza Example Confusion!)" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="1520" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="vs_pizza" value="THE CONFUSION: Both can model Pizza + Toppings!&#xa;&#xa;┌──────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┐&#xa;│  ASPECT              │  BUILDER                    │  DECORATOR                   │&#xa;├──────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤&#xa;│  PATTERN TYPE        │  CREATIONAL                 │  STRUCTURAL                  │&#xa;│                      │  (helps CREATE object)      │  (adds BEHAVIOR)             │&#xa;│                      │                             │                              │&#xa;│  PROBLEM SOLVED      │  Complex object with many   │  Add features dynamically    │&#xa;│                      │  optional parameters        │  at runtime                  │&#xa;│                      │                             │                              │&#xa;│  PIZZA EXAMPLE       │  Pizza pizza = new          │  Pizza pizza = new BasePizza();│&#xa;│  (Builder)           │    PizzaBuilder()           │  pizza = new CheeseDecorator(│&#xa;│                      │      .addCrust('thin')      │            pizza);           │&#xa;│                      │      .addCheese()           │  pizza = new MushroomDecorator(│&#xa;│                      │      .addMushroom()         │            pizza);           │&#xa;│                      │      .build();              │                              │&#xa;│                      │                             │                              │&#xa;│  PIZZA EXAMPLE       │  Need: Base + Cheese        │  Need: Base + Cheese         │&#xa;│  (Decorator)         │  → Use CheeseBuilder        │  → Wrap: new Cheese(base)   │&#xa;│                      │  Need: Base + Mushroom      │  → Wrap: new Mushroom(base) │&#xa;│                      │  → Use MushroomBuilder      │  → Wrap both: new Mushroom( │&#xa;│                      │  Need: Base + Cheese        │         new Cheese(base))    │&#xa;│                      │         + Mushroom          │                              │&#xa;│                      │  → MUST create new Builder! │  → Just wrap both!           │&#xa;│                      │     BaseCheeseMushroom      │     DYNAMIC!                 │&#xa;│                      │     Builder                 │                              │&#xa;│                      │                             │                              │&#xa;│  DYNAMIC SUPPORT     │  ✗ NO! Need predefined      │  ✓ YES! Wrap at runtime      │&#xa;│                      │     builder for each combo  │     (any combination)        │&#xa;│                      │                             │                              │&#xa;│  WHEN TO USE         │  Many optional params       │  Need runtime flexibility    │&#xa;│                      │  Known configurations       │  Add features dynamically    │&#xa;│                      │  Step-by-step creation      │  Avoid subclass explosion    │&#xa;│                      │                             │                              │&#xa;│  EXAMPLE             │  StringBuilder (text build) │  I/O Streams (BufferedReader(│&#xa;│                      │  SQL Query builder          │    FileReader(...)))         │&#xa;│                      │  HTTP Request builder       │  Pizza toppings              │&#xa;└──────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────┘&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;KEY DIFFERENCE:&#xa;  BUILDER: Static (need class for each combination)&#xa;    - CheeseBuilder, MushroomBuilder, CheeseMushroom Builder, ...&#xa;    - If user wants Cheese + Mushroom + Pepperoni → Need NEW Builder class!&#xa;  &#xa;  DECORATOR: Dynamic (wrap at runtime)&#xa;    - new Pepperoni(new Mushroom(new Cheese(basePizza)))&#xa;    - ANY combination supported WITHOUT new classes!&#xa;&#xa;&#xa;SIMPLE RULE:&#xa;  Creating complex object with known configs? → BUILDER&#xa;  Adding features dynamically at runtime?      → DECORATOR" style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#e1d5e7;strokeColor=#9673a6;fontSize=9;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="1560" width="1520" height="780" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <!-- INTERVIEW QUESTIONS -->
        <mxCell id="q_title" value="INTERVIEW QUESTIONS" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;rounded=0;fontSize=16;fontStyle=1" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="2380" width="1520" height="30" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
        <mxCell id="questions" value="Q1: Why not use setters instead of Builder Pattern?&#xa;A1: IMMUTABILITY! Setters allow modification after construction (mutable). Builder creates IMMUTABLE object. Also: (1) Setters break thread-safety, (2) Can't validate state before construction (partial objects exist), (3) Setters scattered across code (no single construction point), (4) Builder provides fluent API (readable). Use setters for mutable beans, Builder for complex immutable objects.&#xa;&#xa;Q2: Real-world examples of Builder Pattern in Java/Spring?&#xa;A2: (1) StringBuilder/StringBuffer (append chaining), (2) Lombok @Builder annotation (generates builder code), (3) Retrofit HTTP client (RequestBuilder), (4) RestTemplate.builder() in Spring, (5) SQL QueryBuilder (JPA Criteria API), (6) Mockito (when().thenReturn() chaining), (7) Stream API (stream.filter().map().collect()). Anywhere you see method chaining for object construction!&#xa;&#xa;Q3: Director is optional. When to use Director?&#xa;A3: Use Director when: (1) Building process has FIXED SEQUENCE (addWall before addRoof), (2) Multiple clients need SAME construction steps, (3) Business logic dictates ORDER (validation rules), (4) Reusable construction recipes. Skip Director when: Client knows what they want (ad-hoc building), No enforced sequence needed, Simple builders. Director = reusable construction template.&#xa;&#xa;Q4: Builder Pattern performance overhead? More objects created?&#xa;A4: Slight overhead (Builder object + Product object). Modern JVMs optimize well. Trade-off: tiny memory/CPU cost vs HUGE gains: (1) Readable code (maintainability), (2) Fewer bugs (no wrong param order), (3) Immutability (thread-safe). For hot paths: (1) Reuse builders (reset method), (2) Object pooling, (3) Benchmark (usually not bottleneck). Readability &gt; micro-optimization.&#xa;&#xa;Q5: How to handle REQUIRED vs OPTIONAL parameters in Builder?&#xa;A5: (1) Mandatory params → Pass in Builder constructor: new StudentBuilder(rollNumber), (2) Optional params → Setter methods, (3) Validation in build() method: if (rollNumber == 0) throw exception, (4) Fluent API: builder.setAge(20).setName('John').build(). Ensures mandatory fields set before build(). Compile-time safety: can't create builder without required params. Runtime safety: build() validates before constructing." style="whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fillColor=#fff2cc;strokeColor=#d6b656;fontSize=10;fontFamily=Courier New;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="2430" width="1520" height="500" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
